180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (This project evaluates how much data-level parallelism improves the performance of k-means clustering. A sequential C implementation serves as the baseline and is compared against two SIMD implementations written in x86-64 assembly: one using XMM registers and another using YMM registers. The parallelization targets the assignment step of Lloyd's algorithm, which computes the distance between every data point and every cluster centroid. The three versions are compared on execution time, speedup, and accuracy  across several numbers of points \(N\) and clusters \(K\). The goal is to show how SIMD register width affects k-means performance and where the speedup stops.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
